

{"id":"d44bd763-8397-47d3-a737-9a9af9585dad","object":"chat.completion","created":1786436973,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"record_id\":\"CA12_CORE_006\",\"decisions\":[{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_01\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_03\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_04\",\"depart_slot\":7,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_02\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_02\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_03\",\"depart_slot\":18,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_03\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_06\",\"depart_slot\":13,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_04\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_07\",\"depart_slot\":5,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_05\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_10\",\"depart_slot\":2,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_06\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_03\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_05\",\"depart_slot\":3,\"charge_blocks\":1}]}","reasoning_content":"We are asked to produce one complete repair for the given instance. The baseline decisions are given, but we must respect fixed UAV assignment and mission order, route compatibility, release/due windows, incoming deadhead, charging, SOC/reserve, corridor/pad/charger capacity, closures, and intervention overrides. We need to optimize lexicographically: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\n\nFirst, understand the instance. The instance has 6 missions: MISSION_01 through MISSION_06. Each mission has a fixed UAV assignment: MISSION_01 -> UAV_03, MISSION_02 -> UAV_02, MISSION_03 -> UAV_01, MISSION_04 -> UAV_01, MISSION_05 -> UAV_01, MISSION_06 -> UAV_03. The order of missions per UAV is fixed? The assignment_order_contract says \"FIXED_UAV_ASSIGNMENT_AND_FIXED_UAV_ORDER\". So missions must be served in the order of their sequence_index per UAV? There are three UAVs: UAV_01, UAV_02, UAV_03. Let's list missions with their UAV and sequence_index:\n\nUAV_01: \n- MISSION_05: sequence_index 0, release_slot 2, due_slot 4, origin VP_02, dest VP_04, path_options PATH_10 (travel_slots 1, energy 0.192978, wind 1, distance 1.2km)\n- MISSION_04: sequence_index 1, release_slot 5, due_slot 22, origin VP_04, dest VP_06, path_options: PATH_01 (distance 4.0, travel 2, energy 0.64326, wind 1), PATH_07 (distance 1.0, travel 2, energy 0.151, wind 0), PATH_09 (distance 1.0, travel 2, energy 0.151, wind 0). Note PATH_07 uses COR_02, PATH_09 uses COR_08. But COR_08 is closed from slot 5 to 28? Intervention says closed_corridor_slots for COR_08 from 5 to 28 inclusive. So PATH_09 is invalid if any travel slot falls in that closure. PATH_09 travel slots: 2 slots, so departure slot + 0 and +1 must avoid closure. Since release is 5, earliest depart is 5, then slots 5 and 6 would be COR_08 which is closed. So PATH_09 cannot be used. PATH_01 uses COR_11 (open?), PATH_07 uses COR_02 (open). So we have options PATH_01 and PATH_07.\n- MISSION_03: sequence_index 2, release_slot 13, due_slot 25, origin VP_06, dest VP_01, path_options PATH_06 (travel_slots 1, energy 0.313589, wind 1, distance 1.95km)\n\nUAV_02:\n- MISSION_02: sequence_index 0, release_slot 18, due_slot 25, origin VP_02, dest VP_05, path_options PATH_03 (distance 1.0, travel 2, energy 0.12543, wind 1) and PATH_08 (incompatible? It says compatible: false, so can't use). So only PATH_03.\n\nUAV_03:\n- MISSION_06: sequence_index 0, release_slot 2, due_slot 8, origin VP_02, dest VP_04, path_options PATH_02 (travel 8, energy 1.356) and PATH_05 (travel 2, energy 0.150516). PATH_02 uses COR_03, PATH_05 uses COR_01. Both are open? COR_03 no closures, COR_01 has capacity override: capacity 1 at slot 2. So at slot 2, only 1 UAV can use COR_01. Since multiple UAVs might use it, we need to be careful. Also UAV_01 MISSION_05 uses PATH_10 which uses COR_01? PATH_10: corridor_ids COR_01, travel 1 slot, energy 0.192978. So MISSION_05 uses COR_01 at departure slot (which is release 2? Actually release 2, so earliest depart slot 2? But MISSION_05 release is 2, due 4, so it must depart at slot 2 or 3? travel 1 slot, so if depart 2, arrival slot 2? travel_slots=1 means it occupies slot [depart_slot, depart_slot]? Actually edge_segment duration_slots=1, offset=0, so it uses the departure slot itself? In typical slot representation, travel occupies the duration slots starting at depart_slot. So if depart 2, travel slots cover 2 (for 1 slot). MISSION_06 PATH_05 travel slots 2, so depart_slot will be some slot, then travel occupies two consecutive slots.\n\nSo we need to schedule missions respecting order per UAV, release/due windows, energy SOC, charging, etc.\n\nWe have initial SOC for each UAV given, but also initial_soc_overrides for UAV_03: initial_soc_kwh 1.690916 (instead of 2.740916). UAV_01 initial_soc 10.67368, UAV_02 initial_soc 7.5, UAV_03 initial_soc 1.690916.\n\nThe UAV parameters:\nUAV_01: battery capacity 22.0, empty_energy_per_km 0.145, payload_energy_per_km_kg 0.012, charge_kwh_per_block 0.95, reserve 2.5, travel_time_coefficient 0.86, start VP_02, available slot 2.\nUAV_02: capacity 16.0, empty_energy 0.1, payload_energy 0.026, charge 1.2, reserve 1.5, coeff 1.0, start VP_02, available slot 2.\nUAV_03: capacity 16.0, empty 0.1, payload_energy 0.026, charge 1.2, reserve 1.5, coeff 1.0, start VP_02, available slot 2.\n\nAll payloads are 0.5 kg for all missions.\n\nEnergy consumption: For a given path, the energy_kwh provided includes both empty and payload? Usually the path energy is precomputed? The path_options provide energy_kwh. So we can use that as the total flight energy for that mission on that path, no need to compute separately. The payload_kg is 0.5, so we can assume the path's energy accounts for that. But careful: the path energy might be empty+payload, or maybe it's provided. The task says: \"edge_segments have wind_level and duration_slots\" and path has energy_kwh. So we can directly use the given energy_kwh for the service flight.\n\nFor deadhead: incoming_deadhead is null for all missions, so no deadhead leg. So the energy order is: incoming deadhead (none) -> service flight -> reserve check -> turnaround -> post-service charge.\n\nSo each mission only has service flight energy, then reserve check.\n\nThe normative order: we deduct service flight energy from battery, then check reserve: remaining energy must be >= reserve_kwh. Then we apply turnaround (charging or waiting? No, turnaround is just time? turnaaround_slots=1, meaning after service flight, the UAV waits 1 slot before it can be used for next mission or charging? Actually turnaround is the time before post-service charge can start? The description: \"destination turnaround -> post-service charge\". So after service flight arrives, there is a turnaround time (1 slot) before charging can begin. That time the UAV is at the destination, maybe occupying pad? Need to check pad capacity? There is pad_capacity_by_slot_default: each vertiport has 2 pads. So during turnaround, the UAV occupies a pad? And during post-service charge, it also occupies a charger and probably a pad? The charger and pad capacities are separate. Charger capacity default 2 per vertiport, pad capacity 2. So each mission will occupy a pad at destination during turnaround and charging? Typically, a UAV at a vertiport after landing will need a pad until it finishes turnaround and possibly moves to a charger. The charger capacity applies to the charging slot. We need to ensure that at each slot, the number of UAVs charging at a vertiport does not exceed charger capacity (2), and the number of UAVs on pads (including during turnaround and maybe charging? Usually charging occupies a charger, but the UAV might still be on a pad? But pad capacity and charger capacity are separate resources. In many UAV scheduling problems, the pad is needed for landing and takeoff, but while charging, it might remain on a pad or a dedicated charging spot. The task says \"charger capacity applies to every occupied post-service charging slot at the destination.\" and \"pad capacity\" is a resource. We must respect pad_capacity_by_slot_default (2 per VP). So most likely, during turnaround (1 slot) the UAV occupies a pad, and during post-service charge, it occupies a charger and possibly a pad. But the baseline scheduling likely handles resource constraints, and we are to repair while minimizing changes. Since the intervention may have overrides for pad/charger capacity, but here there are none except initial SOC overrides and closed corridors. So pad and charger capacities are default 2.\n\nLet's check the baseline decisions:\n\nBaseline:\nMISSION_01: UAV_03, depart_slot 6, path_id PATH_04, held false, charge_blocks 0.\nMISSION_02: UAV_02, depart_slot 18, path_id PATH_03, held false, charge_blocks 0.\nMISSION_03: UAV_01, depart_slot 13, path_id PATH_06, held false, charge_blocks 0.\nMISSION_04: UAV_01, depart_slot 5, path_id PATH_09, held false, charge_blocks 0. PATH_09 uses COR_08, which is closed in intervention slots 5-28. So baseline would be invalid due to closed corridor. That's a perturbation. We need to repair.\nMISSION_05: UAV_01, depart_slot 2, path_id PATH_10, held false, charge_blocks 0.\nMISSION_06: UAV_03, depart_slot 2, path_id PATH_05, held false, charge_blocks 0.\n\nIn baseline, MISSION_05 and MISSION_06 both depart slot 2. MISSION_05 is UAV_01, MISSION_06 is UAV_03. They both start at VP_02. MISSION_05 uses PATH_10 (COR_01, travel 1 slot). MISSION_06 uses PATH_05 (COR_01, travel 2 slots). Both use COR_01. There is a capacity override: corridor COR_01 capacity is reduced to 1 at slot 2. So at slot 2, only one UAV can be in COR_01. In baseline, both depart at slot 2, which would violate the override capacity of 1. So that likely invalidates baseline as well? The intervention includes \"corridor_capacity_overrides\": [{\"capacity\":1,\"corridor_id\":\"COR_01\",\"slot\":2}]. So slot 2, COR_01 capacity is 1 instead of default 2. So if both UAV_01 and UAV_03 depart at slot 2 on COR_01, it's a violation. So baseline is invalid, thus we need to repair to avoid this conflict.\n\nThus we need to reschedule missions respecting all constraints and the fixed order per UAV. The baseline may have held status false for all, but we can hold missions if needed (held=true). But the lexicographic objective minimizes urgent holds first, then total holds, etc. So holding any mission is costly, but if no feasible schedule exists without holds, we might hold. We should try to avoid holds if possible by adjusting depart slots and paths.\n\nFirst, analyze energy constraints for each UAV.\n\nUAV_01: MISSION_05 (seq0), MISSION_04 (seq1), MISSION_03 (seq2). Starting SOC 10.67368 kWh, capacity 22.0, reserve 2.5, charge_kwh_per_block 0.95. Turnaround 1 slot at destination after each mission. Post-service charge can be added (charge_blocks integer up to max 3) after turnaround. The charge energy added cannot exceed battery capacity - current charge after reserve? Actually the SOC after service flight must be >= reserve. Charging adds charge_kwh_per_block * charge_blocks, but final SOC cannot exceed battery capacity. We can choose charge_blocks for each mission except the last? Or we can charge after MISSION_05 at VP_04, after MISSION_04 at VP_06, and maybe after MISSION_03? Actually MISSION_03 goes to VP_01, maybe we can charge there but it's last so not needed? But we can still charge, but energy won't be used. To minimize energy, we want to charge only as needed to enable subsequent missions.\n\nThe order: MISSION_05 -> MISSION_04 -> MISSION_03. So we need enough energy for MISSION_05, then after that we can charge at VP_04 (dest of M05) before M04, but M04 starts from VP_04 (origin of M04 is VP_04, dest of M05 is VP_04, so it's the same location, no deadhead). So after M05, UAV_01 is at VP_04, then after turnaround and possible charge, it can start M04. M04 goes to VP_06, then M03 starts at VP_06 to VP_01. So sequence is VP_02 (start) -> M05 to VP_04 -> M04 to VP_06 -> M03 to VP_01.\n\nPath options and energies:\nM05: PATH_10 (COR_01, 1 slot, 0.192978 kWh, wind 1)\nM04: PATH_01 (4.0km, 2 slots, 0.64326 kWh, wind 1), PATH_07 (1.0km, 2 slots, 0.151 kWh, wind 0)\nM03: PATH_06 (1.95km, 1 slot, 0.313589 kWh, wind 1)\n\nWe must respect release slots: M05 release 2, due 4. So M05 must depart at slot >=2 and finish (arrival?) by due_slot? The due_slot is when the mission must be completed (arrival? Usually due_slot is the latest arrival slot). So M05 departure + travel_slots - 1 (if travel occupies departure slot as first slot) must be <= due_slot. travel_slots=1, so arrival slot = depart_slot. So depart_slot must be between 2 and 4 inclusive. M05 due 4, so depart could be 2,3,4. But release 2, so earliest depart 2.\n\nM04 release 5, due 22. Travel slots 2, so earliest arrival = depart_slot + 1 (if depart at slot d, travel slots are d and d+1, arrival at d+1? Actually with travel_slots=2, arrival occurs after the second slot, so the landing slot is d+1. The due_slot likely is the latest slot for arrival. So if due is 22, then d+1 <=22 => d <=21. So depart can be from 5 to 21.\n\nM03 release 13, due 25, travel 1, so depart 13 to 25.\n\nNow timing: After M05, UAV needs turnaround 1 slot before charging or next mission. If we don't charge, can we start M04 immediately after turnaround? M05 arrives at slot d (if same slot), then turnaround occupies slot d (maybe arrival slot plus turnaround slot? The event order: service flight -> reserve check -> turnaround -> post-service charge. So after arrival, turnaround takes 1 slot. So if arrival at slot t, turnaround occupies slot t (or t to t? Typically, the turnaround time begins at arrival slot and lasts 1 full slot, so the UAV is unavailable for next mission until slot t+1? Actually if turnaround is 1 slot, and we consider that the UAV is busy during that slot, then the next mission can depart no earlier than t+1? But departure for next mission would need to be at a slot where the UAV is available. So if M05 arrival at slot a, then turnaround at slot a (occupies pad), and then after turnaround, the UAV can start next mission at slot a+1 or later? But the next mission departure slot must be at least a+1? Or does turnaround happen in the slot after arrival? Usually, if travel takes 1 slot, arrival is at that slot, then the same slot might be used for turning around? This is ambiguous. In typical slot-based scheduling, if travel_slots=1, and departure at d, the flight occupies slot d, and arrival is at the end of slot d. Then turnaround might be required in the next slot d+1, making the UAV ready at d+2. But the specification says turnaround_slots=1. The baseline schedule: MISSION_05 depart 2, arrival 2 (since 1 slot). Then MISSION_04 depart 5. That leaves a gap: slot 2 arrival, turnaround maybe slot 2 or 3? Actually baseline depart 5 means between M05 arrival slot 2 and M04 depart 5, there are slots 3,4. That might include turnaround and charging? Baseline had charge_blocks=0 for M05, so no charging. So turnaround likely takes 1 slot, and then the UAV is ready. But if turnaround takes slot 2 (arrival slot), then ready at slot 3. Depart 5 implies some waiting. So the model might consider that after arrival, a turnaround slot is required before the UAV can be used again. So the earliest next departure would be arrival_slot + 1 (if turnaround is exactly the next slot)? If arrival at d, turnaround at d, then ready d+1? But then depart could be d+1. Baseline has M05 arrival 2, M04 depart 5, so they didn't minimize gap. So maybe the turnaround slot is after arrival, making the UAV busy for that slot. So arrival at d, turnaround during slot d+1, ready at d+2. But then depart 5 (slot 5) would be after d+2=4, ok. Later we'll need to decide.\n\nFrom the baseline: M05 (depart 2, travel 1 -> arrival 2), M04 (depart 5, travel 2 -> arrival 6). Gap of 3 slots (3,4,5? arrival 2, turnaround perhaps slot 3, then ready 4? But M04 departs 5, so a bit of idle). They had charge_blocks 0 for M05. So it's plausible that turnaround occupies the slot immediately after arrival. Let's assume: after arrival at slot a, the UAV must spend 1 turnaround slot (slot a+1) before it can be assigned to another mission or charge. If charging, the post-service charge occurs after turnaround, so charge slots would begin at a+2? Need to see baseline for other missions. MISSION_01: UAV_03 depart 6, travel 1 (PATH_04 1 slot) -> arrival 6. MISSION_06: UAV_03 depart 2, travel 2 (PATH_05) -> arrival 3. Then M01 depart 6. So between M06 arrival 3 and M01 departure 6, there are slots 4,5 plus turnaround. M06 charge_blocks 0, so no charge. So turnaround likely occupies slot 4, ready at 5, depart 6 (one idle). So that pattern: turnaround slot = arrival_slot+1. So next mission depart slot >= arrival_slot+2? Actually if turnaround finishes after slot arrival+1, then UAV is free starting slot arrival+2. So earliest next depart is arrival+2. For M06 arrival 3, arrival+2=5, but baseline M01 depart 6, ok. So we'll adopt: arrival_slot = depart_slot + travel_slots - 1. Then turnaround occupies slot arrival_slot+1. The UAV is available for next departure at slot departure_next >= arrival_slot+2. For charging: if we charge, the charge blocks start after turnaround, i.e., at slot arrival_slot+2, and each charge block occupies one slot, and the UAV cannot be used until after charging. So if we have charge_blocks = c, then the UAV is free at slot arrival_slot+2 + c. So next departure slot >= arrival_slot+2+c. Also, we must consider pad and charger capacity: during turnaround, the UAV occupies a pad at the destination vertiport. During charging, it occupies a charger (and possibly a pad, but charger and pad are separate). The pad might be needed for landing/takeoff, but likely the UAV remains on a pad during turnaround, but during charging it might move to a charger, freeing the pad? In many air mobility models, the pad is used for takeoff/landing and possibly for parking, while charging happens at a separate parking spot with charger. So we need to check capacity constraints: pad capacity at destination for turnaround slot (arrival+1) must not exceed pad_capacity (2). For charging, each charging slot uses a charger; after charging, the UAV might need a pad to depart? Departure also uses a pad at origin for takeoff. But origin pad usage for departure may occupy a pad slot. Typically, departure uses the pad at departure slot. We need to respect pad capacity at origin for departure. But baseline may have considered pad constraints; since pad capacity default is 2, and max simultaneous UAVs at a vertiport is limited. With only 3 UAVs total, 2 pads per VP should be ample unless multiple UAVs are at same VP simultaneously. We'll keep an eye.\n\nAlso corridor capacity: each segment of a path uses a corridor in specific slots. The path has edge_segments with slots relative to departure. For example, PATH_10: corridor COR_01, duration 1, offset 0, so occupied at slot = depart_slot + offset. So we need to check corridor capacity overrides and closures. The closure for COR_08 from slot 5 to 28 makes PATH_09 impossible. For PATH_07 using COR_02, no closure, open. For PATH_01 using COR_11, no closure? COR_11 is not listed in closures, so open.\n\nNow, we need to produce a repair that meets all constraints and optimizes lexicographically: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy. The baseline decisions had some choices; we can change path, depart_slot, charge_blocks, and possibly hold. We must respect fixed UAV assignment and order per UAV. So UAV_01 must serve M05, M04, M03 in that order. UAV_02 serves M02 only. UAV_03 serves M06, M01 in that order (sequence_index 0 and 1). All missions are present.\n\nWe need to minimize holds. So we want held false for all if possible. Let's see if a feasible schedule exists.\n\nLet's start with UAV_02: only MISSION_02. Release 18, due 25, path PATH_03 travel 2, energy 0.12543 kWh. Starting SOC 7.5, reserve 1.5, capacity 16. So energy consumption 0.12543, after service, remaining >=1.5. So 7.5-0.12543=7.37457 > 1.5, ok. No need to charge before (no previous mission). Departure slot must be >=18, arrival = depart_slot+1 (travel 2 -> departure slot d, travel slots d and d+1, arrival d+1). Due 25, so arrival d+1 <=25 -> d<=24. So depart slots 18..24. But we also need to check turnaound after arrival (not needed since it's last). No conflict with other UAVs except maybe corridor capacity: PATH_03 uses COR_09, duration 2, offset 0. COR_09 capacity default 2, no overrides. So any depart 18-24 is fine. To minimize tardiness? Tardiness: arrival after due? Weighted tardiness, so we want to avoid being late. If we depart 18, arrival 19, due 25, so early, no tardiness. So we can set depart 18, held false, charge_blocks 0.\n\nUAV_03: missions M06 (seq0) and M01 (seq1). Start VP_02, initial SOC 1.690916 (overridden), capacity 16, reserve 1.5, charge_kwh_per_block 1.2. M06 path options: PATH_02 (travel 8, energy 1.356) or PATH_05 (travel 2, energy 0.150516). M01 path only PATH_04 (travel 1, energy 0.0904). M06 release 2, due 8, so if travel 2 (PATH_05), departure d, arrival d+1, due 8 => d+1<=8 => d<=7, and d>=2. If travel 8 (PATH_02), arrival d+7, due<=8 => d+7<=8 => d<=1, but release 2, so impossible. So PATH_02 is infeasible due to due date. So M06 must use PATH_05. Then M01 release 6, due 20, travel 1 (PATH_04) so departure d2>=6, arrival d2, due 20. Sequence: M06 then M01. After M06 arrival at VP_04 (dest of M06 is VP_04), then M01 origin is VP_04, so no deadhead. So M06 arrives at VP_04 at slot d+1 (where d is M06 depart). Then turnaround 1 slot (d+2). After that, M01 can depart at d+2 or later, but must be >=6. Also energy: initial SOC 1.690916, M06 energy 0.150516, remaining after service = 1.5404 kWh. Reserve is 1.5, so 1.5404 >= 1.5, barely ok. But we also need to check reserve immediately after service flight. So SOC after M06 = 1.690916 - 0.150516 = 1.5404, which is >= 1.5, ok. Then after turnaround, we can charge at VP_04 before M01. M01 energy 0.0904. Without charging, remaining after M01 would be 1.54 - 0.0904 = 1.4496, which is less than reserve 1.5, so not allowed. So we must charge after M06 to have enough energy for M01. We can charge using charge_blocks for M06. Charge blocks occur at VP_04 after M06 turnaround. Each charge block adds 1.2 kWh, but cannot exceed battery capacity 16. So we can charge 1 block, add 1.2, SOC becomes min(1.5404+1.2, 16)=2.7404. Then M01 service: 2.7404 - 0.0904 = 2.65, which is >1.5. So feasible. But if we charge 0 blocks, then M01 would violate reserve. So we need charge_blocks >=1 for M06. Max 3 blocks, but 1 is enough. Could also do 2 or 3, but excess might violate energy upper bound? No, just cannot exceed capacity. 1 block is fine.\n\nNow timing: M06 departure d1 (>=2, <=7), arrival a1 = d1+1. Turnaround at d1+2. Charging after turnaround: if we charge 1 block, at slot d1+2 (the charge block) and then UAV free at d1+3. So M01 earliest departure d2 >= d1+3. M01 release 6, so we need d2>=6, also d2>=d1+3. So if d1+3 <=6, then d2 can be 6. Since d1 max 7, d1+3 max 10, so if d1=3, d1+3=6 => can depart 6; if d1=2, d1+3=5, earliest possible 5 but must be >=6, so d2=6. So we can choose d1 and d2 accordingly. Also M01 due 20, so d2 <=20, which is fine. Now corridor capacity: M06 uses PATH_05: COR_01, duration 2, offset 0, so occupies slots d1 and d1+1. M05 (UAV_01) also uses COR_01 at departure d5 (for M05) with PATH_10, duration 1, offset 0. Intervention: COR_01 capacity at slot 2 is 1. So we must avoid both UAVs using COR_01 at slot 2 simultaneously. If we set M06 d1=2, then it occupies slots 2 and 3. M05 may also need to use COR_01, maybe at slot 2? M05 release 2, due 4, travel 1, so it could depart 2, 3, or 4. If M05 departs 2, it would use COR_01 at slot 2, conflict. If M05 departs 3, then slot 2 is free? But M06 still occupies slot 3 with COR_01? M06 occupies slot 2 and 3, so COR_01 would be used at slot 3 by M06. M05 departing 3 would use COR_01 at slot 3, still conflict. If M05 departs 4, uses slot 4, M06 uses 2 and 3, no direct conflict. So we could set M06 d1=2, M05 d1=4. That works. But also consider M06 departure d1 could be 3, then M06 uses slots 3,4, and M05 could depart 2 (using slot 2) no conflict, or depart 4 conflict. So we need to coordinate. Also M01 uses PATH_04 with COR_05, no conflicts. So we can find slots.\n\nAll that matters is we can schedule without holds. So UAV_03 feasible with charge_blocks=1. But baseline had charge_blocks 0 for M06 and 0 for M01, which would be infeasible due to energy. So we will set charge_blocks=1 for M06.\n\nNow UAV_01: M05, M04, M03. Start SOC 10.67368, capacity 22, reserve 2.5, charge 0.95. M05: PATH_10 energy 0.192978, M04: energy depends on path (0.64326 for PATH_01 or 0.151 for PATH_07). M03: 0.313589. Also need to meet release/due and order. M05 release 2, due 4, travel 1. So depart d5 in {2,3,4}. Arrival a5 = d5. Then turnaround at d5+1, available for M04 departure at d5+2? (if no charge) or after charge. M05 we could charge at VP_04, but we might not need to because starting SOC is high. SOC after M05: 10.67368 - 0.192978 = 10.480702, reserve 2.5 ok. After M04: if PATH_07 (0.151), then 10.480702 - 0.151 = 10.329702, then M03: 10.329702 - 0.313589 = 10.016113, reserve 2.5 ok. So no charging needed, all energy ample. If we use PATH_01 (0.64326), after M04: 10.480702 - 0.64326 = 9.837442, then M03: 9.837442 - 0.313589 = 9.523853, still fine. So charge_blocks can be 0 for all. But we must respect closure of COR_08; PATH_09 is invalid. So M04 must use PATH_07 or PATH_01. PATH_07 uses COR_02, no closure, wind 0, energy 0.151. PATH_01 uses COR_11, no closure, wind 1, energy 0.64326. Which is better? Objectives: minimize energy is last lexicographic obj